========================================================================================================
DOES THE WALK EARN ITS CIRCUIT ANYWHERE? v20 finished + the wide-R regime
========================================================================================================
  6 paired seeds, 20 epochs, k_steps=15, QPE k=4.
  Arms share one sensing call per block-epoch: the gradient is IDENTICAL.
  gradstep is bounded by the same box as the walk, so nothing is tuned for it.
  The walk costs 2 circuits/block-epoch, gradstep 1 - so a TIE is a LOSS.

  ===== Heisenberg N=4 | exact -6.4641 | 4 params/block = 16 vertices =====
         R0  shots   s/vtx       walk   boltz0.1   gradstep  walk-boltz  walk-grad    sec
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       wide    256    16.0    -5.7042    -5.3651    -5.5346  -0.3391(4.3s) -0.1696(1.4s)    397
       wide   1024    64.0    -5.6594    -5.7465    -5.7793  +0.0871(0.4s) +0.1199(0.7s)    462

  ===== Heisenberg N=6 | exact -9.9743 | 6 params/block = 64 vertices =====
         R0  shots   s/vtx       walk   boltz0.1   gradstep  walk-boltz  walk-grad    sec
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       wide    256     4.0    -7.7775    -7.9072    -8.2025  +0.1296(0.4s) +0.4249(1.2s)   1520
       wide   1024    16.0    -8.7440    -8.9257    -8.7769  +0.1817(2.4s) +0.0329(0.3s)   1194

  NEGATIVE walk-grad means the WALK reached lower energy, which is the only
  thing that justifies its second circuit. Flat or positive everywhere -
  including at wide R, where the box is multi-modal and the stochastic
  bounded step is supposed to pay - means the walk is REMOVABLE and QLTO is
  a one-circuit gradient estimator feeding a classical step.
  walk-boltz should stay NEGATIVE throughout: that is the positive control,
  and if it is not, the harness is not measuring what it should.
  Read sigma against this project's own record: 1-2 sigma on six seeds has
  reversed twice before, so treat anything under ~3 sigma as a tie.
